{
  "sub_unit_name": "Graph Representation and BFS/DFS",
  "subject_short_code": "DSA100",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Why Study Graphs?",
      "description": "Graphs model relationships between entities and are used in social networks, maps, recommendation systems, networking, AI, and search engines."
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Many real-world problems involve connections rather than linear structures. Graphs help represent these relationships and provide efficient ways to explore connected data.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Real-Life Examples",
      "content": "Facebook friendships, Google Maps routes, airline networks, internet routing, recommendation engines, and dependency graphs are all examples of graph-based systems."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "What is a Graph?"
    },

    {
      "type": "definition-card",
      "term": "Graph",
      "definition": "A non-linear data structure consisting of vertices (nodes) and edges (connections) between those vertices."
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>A graph is represented as G(V, E), where V represents vertices and E represents edges connecting the vertices.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Basic Graph Example",
      "steps": ["      A", "     / \\", "    B---C", "     \\ /", "      D"]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Graph Terminology"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Important Terms",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Vertex (Node)",
          "answer": "An individual entity in the graph."
        },
        {
          "question": "Edge",
          "answer": "A connection between two vertices."
        },
        {
          "question": "Degree",
          "answer": "Number of edges connected to a vertex."
        },
        {
          "question": "Path",
          "answer": "A sequence of connected vertices."
        },
        {
          "question": "Cycle",
          "answer": "A path that starts and ends at the same vertex."
        },
        {
          "question": "Connected Graph",
          "answer": "A graph where every vertex is reachable from every other vertex."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Types of Graphs"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Graph Categories",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Undirected Graph",
          "answer": "Edges do not have direction."
        },
        {
          "question": "Directed Graph (Digraph)",
          "answer": "Edges have specific directions."
        },
        {
          "question": "Weighted Graph",
          "answer": "Edges contain weights or costs."
        },
        {
          "question": "Unweighted Graph",
          "answer": "Edges do not have weights."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Undirected Graph"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Undirected Graph Example",
      "steps": ["A ----- B", "|       |", "|       |", "D ----- C"]
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Key Observation",
      "content": "If A is connected to B, then B is automatically connected to A."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Directed Graph"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Directed Graph Example",
      "steps": ["A → B", "↓   ↓", "D ← C"]
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Key Observation",
      "content": "Direction matters. A → B does not imply B → A."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Graph Representation"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Graphs are commonly represented using Adjacency Matrices and Adjacency Lists.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Adjacency Matrix"
    },

    {
      "type": "definition-card",
      "term": "Adjacency Matrix",
      "definition": "A 2D matrix where matrix[i][j] indicates whether an edge exists between vertices i and j."
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Adjacency Matrix Example",
      "steps": ["    A B C", "A [0 1 1]", "B [1 0 1]", "C [1 1 0]"]
    },

    {
      "type": "comparison-card",
      "left_title": "Advantages",
      "right_title": "Disadvantages",
      "left_points": [
        "Easy edge lookup",
        "Simple implementation",
        "Works well for dense graphs"
      ],
      "right_points": [
        "Consumes O(V²) space",
        "Inefficient for sparse graphs",
        "Wastes memory"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Adjacency Matrix Complexity",
      "time_complexity": "Edge Lookup O(1)",
      "space_complexity": "O(V²)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Adjacency List"
    },

    {
      "type": "definition-card",
      "term": "Adjacency List",
      "definition": "A collection of lists where each vertex stores its neighboring vertices."
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Adjacency List Example",
      "steps": ["A → B, C", "B → A, C", "C → A, B", "D → C"]
    },

    {
      "type": "comparison-card",
      "left_title": "Advantages",
      "right_title": "Disadvantages",
      "left_points": [
        "Memory efficient",
        "Best for sparse graphs",
        "Easy traversal"
      ],
      "right_points": [
        "Edge lookup slower",
        "More pointer references",
        "Less efficient for dense graphs"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Adjacency List Complexity",
      "time_complexity": "Traversal O(V + E)",
      "space_complexity": "O(V + E)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Breadth First Search (BFS)"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>BFS explores a graph level by level. It visits all neighbors of a node before moving deeper into the graph.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "definition-card",
      "term": "BFS",
      "definition": "A graph traversal algorithm that explores vertices level by level using a Queue."
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Core Data Structure",
      "content": "BFS always uses a Queue to keep track of nodes to visit next."
    },

    {
      "type": "process_flow",
      "title": "BFS Algorithm",
      "steps": [
        "Start from Source Vertex",
        "Mark Source as Visited",
        "Add Source to Queue",
        "Remove Front Vertex",
        "Visit All Unvisited Neighbors",
        "Add Neighbors to Queue",
        "Repeat Until Queue Becomes Empty"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "BFS Traversal Example",
      "steps": [
        "Graph:",
        "A → B, C",
        "B → D",
        "C → E",
        "",
        "BFS Order:",
        "A → B → C → D → E"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "code-example",
      "language": "java",
      "title": "BFS Pseudocode",
      "code": "Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();\nq.add(start);\nwhile(!q.isEmpty()) {\n int node = q.poll();\n visit(node);\n}"
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "BFS Complexity",
      "time_complexity": "O(V + E)",
      "space_complexity": "O(V)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Applications of BFS"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Where BFS is Used",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Shortest Path in Unweighted Graph",
          "answer": "Finds minimum number of edges between nodes."
        },
        {
          "question": "Web Crawlers",
          "answer": "Explore websites layer by layer."
        },
        {
          "question": "Social Networks",
          "answer": "Friend recommendation systems."
        },
        {
          "question": "Network Broadcasting",
          "answer": "Spread information level by level."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Depth First Search (DFS)"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>DFS explores as deep as possible along a branch before backtracking.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "definition-card",
      "term": "DFS",
      "definition": "A graph traversal algorithm that explores one path completely before exploring alternative paths."
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Core Data Structure",
      "content": "DFS is typically implemented using Recursion or an explicit Stack."
    },

    {
      "type": "process_flow",
      "title": "DFS Algorithm",
      "steps": [
        "Visit Current Vertex",
        "Mark as Visited",
        "Move to Unvisited Neighbor",
        "Continue Until Dead End",
        "Backtrack",
        "Repeat Until All Nodes Are Visited"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "DFS Traversal Example",
      "steps": [
        "Graph:",
        "A → B, C",
        "B → D",
        "C → E",
        "",
        "Possible DFS Order:",
        "A → B → D → C → E"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "code-example",
      "language": "java",
      "title": "DFS Pseudocode",
      "code": "void dfs(int node){\n visited[node] = true;\n for(neighbor : graph[node]){\n   if(!visited[neighbor]) dfs(neighbor);\n }\n}"
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "DFS Complexity",
      "time_complexity": "O(V + E)",
      "space_complexity": "O(V)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Applications of DFS"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Where DFS is Used",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Cycle Detection",
          "answer": "Detect cycles in graphs."
        },
        {
          "question": "Topological Sorting",
          "answer": "Order tasks with dependencies."
        },
        {
          "question": "Connected Components",
          "answer": "Identify graph clusters."
        },
        {
          "question": "Maze Solving",
          "answer": "Explore paths deeply before backtracking."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "BFS vs DFS"
    },

    {
      "type": "comparison-card",
      "left_title": "BFS",
      "right_title": "DFS",
      "left_points": [
        "Uses Queue",
        "Level-by-level traversal",
        "Finds shortest path",
        "Higher memory usage"
      ],
      "right_points": [
        "Uses Stack/Recursion",
        "Depth-first traversal",
        "Good for exploration",
        "Usually less memory"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Complexity Summary"
    },

    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": ["Operation", "Complexity"],
      "rows": [
        ["Adjacency Matrix Space", "O(V²)"],
        ["Adjacency List Space", "O(V + E)"],
        ["BFS Traversal", "O(V + E)"],
        ["DFS Traversal", "O(V + E)"],
        ["Queue Operations (BFS)", "O(1)"],
        ["Stack Operations (DFS)", "O(1)"]
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "warning-box",
      "title": "Interview Tip",
      "content": "Whenever a graph problem asks for shortest path in an unweighted graph, BFS is usually the first algorithm to consider."
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Interview Insight",
      "description": "Graph Representation, BFS, and DFS are the foundation of advanced graph algorithms such as Dijkstra's Algorithm, Topological Sorting, Connected Components, Minimum Spanning Trees, and Network Flow."
    },

    {
      "type": "revision-card",
      "title": "Quick Revision",
      "points": [
        "Graphs consist of vertices and edges.",
        "Graphs can be directed or undirected.",
        "Adjacency Matrix uses O(V²) space.",
        "Adjacency List uses O(V + E) space.",
        "BFS uses a Queue.",
        "DFS uses Recursion or a Stack.",
        "BFS explores level by level.",
        "DFS explores depth first.",
        "Both BFS and DFS run in O(V + E).",
        "BFS is commonly used for shortest path in unweighted graphs."
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "quiz",
      "question": "Which data structure is used in BFS?",
      "options": ["Stack", "Queue", "Heap", "Trie"],
      "answer": "Queue"
    },

    {
      "type": "quiz",
      "question": "Which traversal explores nodes level by level?",
      "options": ["DFS", "BFS", "Heap Traversal", "Inorder Traversal"],
      "answer": "BFS"
    },

    {
      "type": "quiz",
      "question": "What is the time complexity of BFS on an adjacency list representation?",
      "options": ["O(V²)", "O(E²)", "O(V + E)", "O(log V)"],
      "answer": "O(V + E)"
    }
  ]
}
